          ":question/id": "1938e380-4886-59d0-baa4-d58100804a76",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-69623",
          ":question/problem": "Find $G+H-(H+F)$, if\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$F=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$G=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$H=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G+H-(H+F) & =G+H-H-F \\\\  & =G+\\cancel{H}-\\cancel{H}-F \\\\  & =G-F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G-F & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-2 & 4-(-1) & -5-2 \\\\ 1-3 & -2-4 & 2-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
