          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-69627",
          ":question/problem": "Find $C+V-(N+C)$, if\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 25 \\\\ -57 & -23\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$V=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$N=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C+V-(N+C) & =C+V-N-C \\\\  & =\\cancel{C}+V-N-\\cancel{C} \\\\  & =V-N\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V-N & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5-1 & 3-(-7) \\\\ 6-2 & -4-(-6) \\\\ -1-3 & -8-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        }
