              ":question/id": "25228be0-9b9e-50b6-9435-b95178ff0734",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-1540",
              ":question/problem": "Simplify $-\\left(-p + 2q - \\dfrac{1}{3} \\right)$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "To distribute the negative sign to the terms inside the parentheses, all we have to do is change the sign of each term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (-p + 2q - \\frac{1}{3}) &=  \\\\\n&= p - 2q + \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "f95a482b-4222-5842-b7e3-c39c197df3e6",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Distributing the Negative over More Than Two Terms"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054765,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054493,
              ":question/id": "d95640d6-9f85-565d-9f9e-450ae09f6541",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-1601",
              ":question/problem": "Simplify the expression $-\\left(3n+\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right)$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the negative sign as multiplication by $(-1)\\mathbin$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} - \\left(3n+\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right) \\\\ &= (-1) \\left(3n+\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right) \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the $(-1)$ to each term in the parentheses, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (-1) \\left(3n+\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right) \\\\ &= (-1) (3n)+(-1) \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right) \\\\ &= -3n+ \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\right) \\\\ &= -3n-\\dfrac{1}{2}k \\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "dff2b5c3-149e-5f99-95e2-52d0496a232a",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Distributing the Negative Sign over the Sum of Two Terms"
          }
